      "side": 1,
      "message": "What happens when a Starlark target depends on a builtin target with **2 or more outputs** (e.g., a multi-file `copy`, multi-output `action`, or multi-output binary)?\n\n`Session::run_builtin_rule()` calls `Depset::new_file_depset(outputs, heap, state)`. When `outputs.len() \u003e\u003d 2`, `new_file_depset` creates a new phony (`state.new_phony(...)`, stored in `EvaluatedTarget::phonies`), and downstream `depset(transitive \u003d [dep[DefaultInfo].files])` emits that phony name into Ninja (`depset/src/globals.rs:118`). However, the builtin target\u0027s `build phony/...: phony ...` rule will never be written to Ninja because:\n1. `ninja_target_writer.cc:140` skips `write_phonies()` for `target-\u003eis_builtin()`.\n2. Even without that guard, the builtin target\u0027s own Ninja file can already be written (`Builder::FinalizeItem`) before a downstream Starlark target lazily evaluates it.\n\nThe new test passes only because `shared_library(\"non_starlark\")` happens to have a single output (`outputs.len() \u003d\u003d 1`), hitting the single-file fast path.\n\nTwo possible fixes:\n- Reuse the builtin target\u0027s existing GN `dependency_output()` (phony/stamp already emitted by GN) as the `Depset`\u0027s phony instead of creating a new `state.new_phony()`, or\n- Record lazily-created builtin phonies on `Session` (similar to `mnemonics`) and emit them after all targets are resolved in `NinjaBuildWriter`.\n\nCould we also add a multi-output builtin target (e.g., `copy` or multi-output `action`) to `integration_tests/starlark/` to cover this case?",
      "isAi": true,
      "revId": "594df12d2c7e097d95a5a020049c8967d9706443",
      "serverId": "c360c8e5-7aa8-3905-a7a0-810934766dac"
    },
